/* Logic:

We are given an array containing numbers from `0` to `n`, but one number is missing.

- First, calculate the size of the array and initialize a variable `ans = 0`.
- XOR all numbers from `0` to `n` because this represents the complete range of numbers that should be present.
- Then XOR every element of the given array with the same variable `ans`.
- XOR has an important property: when two identical numbers are XORed, they cancel each other and give `0` (`a ^ a = 0`).
- Since all numbers except the missing number are present in both the complete range and the array, they cancel each other.
- The missing number has no matching pair, so it remains in `ans`.
- Finally, return `ans`, which is the missing number.

Example:

text

```text
Array = [3, 0, 1]
Range = [0, 1, 2, 3]

After XOR cancellation:
0 ^ 0 ^ 1 ^ 1 ^ 3 ^ 3 ^ 2

All pairs cancel, leaving 2.

Answer = 2
```
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {

        // Get the size of the array
        int n = nums.size();

        // Initialize answer with 0
        int ans = 0;

        // XOR all numbers from 0 to n
        // This represents the complete range of numbers
        for(int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            ans ^= i;
        }

        // XOR all elements present in the array
        // Common numbers will cancel each other
        for(int num : nums) {
            ans ^= num;
        }

        // Only the missing number remains
        return ans;
    }
};

